  volume       = 7,
  year	       = 2009,
  abstract     = { ``Torture porn,'' a new extreme subgenre of horror
                  cinema that centers on the attempts of abducted
                  characters to survive torture procedures at the
                  hands of their captors, has fallen prey to
                  criticisms by politicians, pressure groups, and
                  media pundits for what is held to be its potentially
                  damaging sadism and moral vacuity. Seen thus from
                  the limiting perspective of the logic of media
                  effects, this trend in recent horror film looks like
                  moral pollution, but as I argue, seen in terms of
                  Deleuzian affect theory and a model of spectatorship
                  focusing on masochism rather than sadism, it may
                  actually have a transformative and liberating
